WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.690 --> 00:00:06.440
Spirometry is a test of how
well your lungs are working,

00:00:06.440 --> 00:00:09.600
by measuring how fast
and how much air you

00:00:09.600 --> 00:00:12.590
can breathe in and out.

00:00:12.590 --> 00:00:17.490
Normally as you breathe in,
or inhale, air moves freely

00:00:17.490 --> 00:00:20.550
through your
trachea, or windpipe,

00:00:20.550 --> 00:00:24.580
then through large
tubes called bronchi,

00:00:24.580 --> 00:00:29.400
smaller tubes called
the bronchioles,

00:00:29.400 --> 00:00:34.750
and finally into tiny
sacs called alveoli.

00:00:34.750 --> 00:00:37.500
Small blood vessels,
called capillaries,

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:40.700
surround your alveoli.

00:00:42.920 --> 00:00:48.220
Oxygen from the air you breathe
passes into your capillaries.

00:00:48.220 --> 00:00:50.820
Then carbon dioxide
from your body

00:00:50.820 --> 00:00:54.840
passes out of your
capillaries into an alveolus.

00:00:54.840 --> 00:00:56.840
You get rid of
the carbon dioxide

00:00:56.840 --> 00:01:02.800
when you breathe out, or exhale.

00:01:02.800 --> 00:01:05.900
Diseases such as
asthma, bronchitis,

00:01:05.900 --> 00:01:10.220
and pulmonary fibrosis
narrow your bronchioles,

00:01:10.220 --> 00:01:16.900
reducing the amount of
air going into your lungs.

00:01:17.360 --> 00:01:20.550
And diseases such as
lung cancer and emphysema

00:01:20.550 --> 00:01:24.480
damage your alveoli,
reducing the amount of oxygen

00:01:24.480 --> 00:01:29.100
in your blood.

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:35.100
These diseases can make it
hard for you to breathe.

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:39.100
Your doctor may recommend
a spirometry test

00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:42.450
to identify a disease
in your lungs,

00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:46.450
check the severity of your
existing lung disease,

00:01:46.450 --> 00:01:48.870
or to determine if the
medications you take

00:01:48.870 --> 00:01:51.400
are helping.

00:01:52.230 --> 00:01:54.350
During the test,
your caregiver will

00:01:54.350 --> 00:01:57.140
use a device called
a spirometer.

00:01:57.140 --> 00:01:59.310
A spirometer is a
machine that measures

00:01:59.310 --> 00:02:02.710
the air you breathe out.

00:02:02.710 --> 00:02:05.090
Before you take the
spectrometry test,

00:02:05.090 --> 00:02:08.120
you will sit in a comfortable
chair with your feet

00:02:08.120 --> 00:02:09.979
flat on the floor.

00:02:09.979 --> 00:02:13.960
If you have dentures, you
may be asked to remove them.

00:02:13.960 --> 00:02:16.360
For best results,
you will be advised

00:02:16.360 --> 00:02:22.900
to follow your caregiver's
instructions exactly.

00:02:23.170 --> 00:02:25.870
To start, you will
raise your head and chin

00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:30.100
so that you can breathe easily.

00:02:30.310 --> 00:02:32.450
Next, you will place
a clip on your nose

00:02:32.450 --> 00:02:37.800
to prevent air from coming
out of your nostrils.

00:02:38.590 --> 00:02:41.920
Then, you'll take a deep breath,
filling your lungs completely

00:02:41.920 --> 00:02:44.080
with air, and hold it.

00:02:44.080 --> 00:02:47.570
You will place the spirometer's
mouthpiece between your teeth

00:02:47.570 --> 00:02:50.800
and tightly seal your
lips surround it.

00:02:50.800 --> 00:02:53.730
Finally, you will blast
the air out of your lungs

00:02:53.730 --> 00:02:57.860
as hard and as fast as you
can, continuing to breathe out

00:02:57.860 --> 00:03:03.300
until your caregiver
tells you to stop.

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:08.540
If you are an adult, you will
blow for at least six seconds.

00:03:08.540 --> 00:03:14.900
Children 10 years old and under
will blow for three seconds.

00:03:15.310 --> 00:03:18.210
You will need to perform the
spirometry test correctly

00:03:18.210 --> 00:03:22.360
three times to get
accurate results.

